WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.879
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.879 --> 00:00:11.630
In 1989 Thai national Chamoy Thipyaso received
the longest prison sentence ever, 141,078

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.360
years.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.730
Her crime?

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:22.280
She ran a Ponzi scheme that defrauded more
than 16,000 people of $204 million.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:27.270
You've heard of a Ponzi scheme but do you
know where the phrase came from?

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:35.120
Meet the con man that gave conning its very
own name, the suave Charles Ponzi.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.540
Born in Italy, he moved to America and by
the age of forty he had earned a reputation

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.690
for being one of the world's most infamous
swindlers.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:46.850
He arrived in America with just $2.50 in his
pocket.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:50.780
He setup an investment company called the
"Securities Exchange Company".

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:57.330
He promised anyone who invested, a 50% return
on their investment within a month.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:03.719
In the first month, 18 people invested a total
of $1,800 into the company.

00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:08.990
And sure enough Ponzi delivered on his promise
of a 50% return.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.430
But how?

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:15.650
Well, he wasn't actually investing any money
at all.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:21.110
He simply took the money from new investors
and gave it to old investors.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.860
This is essentially the idea of what we now
know as a Ponzi scheme.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:30.240
Whilst the idea sounds great for investors
who got in there early, Ponzi schemes are

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.310
not sustainable and all of them will eventually
collapse.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:39.900
At which point, the schemer will take everyone's
money and do a runner.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:45.079
Just 6 months after Charles Ponzi setup his
company he was earning a staggering one million

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:47.229
dollars per day.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:50.470
Of course, the scheme inevitably collapsed.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:57.310
By this point a whopping 75% of Boston's police
force had already been investigating Ponzi

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:03.940
for a while, suspecting his miraculous investment
scheme had a few legal holes.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:09.919
A short while later he was sentenced to just
five years in prison.

00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:11.819
Investors lost millions of dollars.

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:16.680
And so the infamous Ponzi scheme had been
born.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:22.129
Many such schemes still go on to this very
day around the world.

00:02:22.129 --> 00:02:28.549
Charles Ponzi was a confident man, he was
a con-man.

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:33.459
But one would have to be immensely confident
to achieve what Czech-Republic born Count

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:40.620
Victor Lustig did; he sold the Eiffel tower,
twice.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.340
The Eiffel tower wasn't always the gleaming
beacon of Parisian culture that we know it

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:47.139
as today.

00:02:47.139 --> 00:02:53.480
In 1925 the Eiffel Tower was becoming rundown,
the costs to maintain it were far too great.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:59.120
Newspapers were soon reporting about how derelict
the tower had become and there were even rumours

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.650
that it was soon to be demolished.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.299
Victor Lustig saw opportunity.

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:10.650
He had a professional forger create fake government
stationary and gathered together six scrap

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:16.739
metal dealers at a confidential meeting in
one of Paris' most prestigious hotels, Hôtel

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:18.079
de Crillon.

00:03:18.079 --> 00:03:22.760
Under the guise of a government employee he
told the scrap dealers that due to the overwhelming

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:28.379
expense of maintaining the Eiffel tower, the
city wanted to sell it for scrap metal.

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:32.239
Lustig even took the men in a limousine tour
around the Tower.

00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:39.129
One of the scrap dealers, a Mr Andre Poisson,
fell for the grand show that Lustig had cleverly

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:40.129
curated.

00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:45.529
I mean, if your name is literally Mr. Fish,
you're going to be rather gullible, aren't

00:03:45.529 --> 00:03:46.529
you.

00:03:46.529 --> 00:03:55.060
Poisson made a bid for the tower and later
handed Lustig a briefcase full of cash.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.519
Lustig then swiftly hopped on a train to Vienna.

00:03:58.519 --> 00:04:05.610
So did Poisson go to the police after he realised
he had bought absolutely nothing?

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:11.479
Nope, he was far too embarrassed and said
nothing to anyone.

00:04:11.479 --> 00:04:17.519
No more than a month later and Lustig returned
to Paris and was once again in a hotel with

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:24.669
six more scrap dealers and yes, for a second
time, he managed to fraudulently sell the

00:04:24.669 --> 00:04:26.460
Eiffel tower.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:32.020
This time the police were informed, but by
that point Lustig had already fled.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:38.470
Lustig was so confident that he later went
on to scam infamous gangster Al Capone out

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:42.830
of $50,000 in a fraudulent stock deal.

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:49.360
Lustig isn't the only confidence trickster
that has successfully sold a national icon.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:58.430
An Indian man simply known as "Natwarlal"
sold the Taj Mahal, not once, not twice, but

00:04:58.430 --> 00:04:59.550
three times.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:05.651
Natwarlal was the stereotypical con man, he
used more than 50 aliases and was said to

00:05:05.651 --> 00:05:07.800
be a master of disguises.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:13.870
He was also highly skilled at forging signatures,
it is said that with a single glance at any

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:19.330
signature, he could instantaneously replicate
it like a pro.

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:25.159
Natwarlal would pose as a government official
and approach wealthy tourists offering to

00:05:25.159 --> 00:05:29.389
sell them famous Indian monuments for thousands
of rupees.

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:36.449
Most people would find this incredibly suspicious,
yet despite this, Natwarlal managed to successfully

00:05:36.449 --> 00:05:40.099
sell the Taj Mahal three times.

00:05:40.099 --> 00:05:46.539
He also sold the Red Fort and even the President
of India's very own home, the Rashtrapati

00:05:46.539 --> 00:05:47.639
Bhavan.

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:53.720
The prolific con man was wanted in 8 states
for over 100 different cases of theft and

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:54.720
fraud.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.699
He was eventually caught and sentenced to
113 years in prison.

00:05:59.699 --> 00:06:08.530
But astonishingly he was locked up eight times
and all eight times he managed to escape.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:15.180
It's said that he only served 20 of the 113
years he was sentenced to.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.870
He was last seen in 1996 at the age of 84.

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:24.569
The police managed to find him once again
and arrested him, but sure enough he gave

00:06:24.569 --> 00:06:27.860
them the slip and he was never seen again.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:33.620
His brother claims he died later that year,
but his lawyer claims he didn't die until

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:35.990
2009.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:37.539
Nobody knows for sure.

00:06:37.539 --> 00:06:44.370
Although some say he may even still be alive
today, out there, somewhere.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:51.029
Now, there's pretending to sell the Eiffel
tower and the Taj Mahal and then there's pretending

00:06:51.029 --> 00:06:58.479
to sell something that doesn't exist, can't
be seen and nobody has ever heard of.

00:06:58.479 --> 00:07:02.009
This actually happened, several times.

00:07:02.009 --> 00:07:08.120
In the early 19th century Scottish native
Gregor MacGregor told all his acquaintances

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:14.240
that he was prince of a land called Poyais
along Hondura's Black River.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:19.720
He claimed his mysterious land was a little
larger than Wales and bragged about its incredible

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:21.160
maize harvests.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:25.629
He said that the water is the purest and most
refreshing in all the world.

00:07:25.629 --> 00:07:28.759
The riverbeds are lined with chunks of gold,
he said.

00:07:28.759 --> 00:07:33.960
The forest full of rare game and the trees
grow the most exotic fruits.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:40.250
This edenic vision of a rich, fertile land,
full of treasures was a stark contrast to

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:44.199
the wet, bleakness of the rugged Scottish
landscape.

00:07:44.199 --> 00:07:49.809
So then it's no surprise that when MacGregor
asked investors to contribute money to help

00:07:49.809 --> 00:07:55.469
him gather resources from his homeland, promising
to return the investors' initial investments

00:07:55.469 --> 00:08:00.150
many times over, that they snapped up the
offer in their droves.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:05.449
At the time, Central and South America where
upcoming regions and were already popular

00:08:05.449 --> 00:08:07.490
with investors.

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:12.059
MacGregor even drew up maps of the island
and commissioned artists to draw pictures

00:08:12.059 --> 00:08:18.559
of his made-up homeland, which he could use
as further proof to investors.

00:08:18.559 --> 00:08:25.570
MacGregor gathered together £1.3 million
of investments, the equivalent of £3.6 billion

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.039
today.

00:08:28.039 --> 00:08:35.560
In September 1822, seven ships set sail with
250 settlers and some of MacGregor's investors,

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:40.430
so that they could set foot upon Poyais and
see the mystical land they had ploughed so

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:43.829
much money into, with their very own eyes.

00:08:43.829 --> 00:08:48.491
Of course the co-ordinates they were given
were pulled out of thin air and after a two

00:08:48.491 --> 00:08:55.269
month's voyage at sea they did find a random
desolate part of Honduras, but it certainly

00:08:55.269 --> 00:09:00.760
wasn't anything like the beautiful, edenic
paradise of Poyais that had been described

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:02.130
to them.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:08.010
They attempted to settle there anyway, since
they had little choice, but within a few weeks,

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:11.100
the vast majority of them had died.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:17.829
Word soon returned to Scotland of the investor's
demise and MacGregor skipped town.

00:09:17.829 --> 00:09:23.890
He fled to France and he felt so guilty about
sending 250 Scottish people to perish at sea

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:29.130
in search of a made up island, that he did
it all again.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:33.580
Within just a few months he had swindled a
group of French investors out of their hard

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:34.740
earned cash.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:39.680
And he assembled a new group of French settlers
and investors ready to embark on another long

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.089
sea voyage to setup their new home on Poyais.

00:09:43.089 --> 00:09:50.350
But the French authorities weren't quite as
laid back as the Scottish and they created

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:54.880
a commission to investigate whether Poyais
actually existed.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:10:01.100
Of course when the French authorities discovered
the rather enormous and glaring holes in MacGregor's

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.529
story they swiftly threw him in jail.

00:10:04.529 --> 00:10:06.920
But did that stop him?

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:13.111
No, even after he was convicted and everyone
knew his fairytale island was a lie, MacGregor

00:10:13.111 --> 00:10:21.449
continued to sell non-existent land to the
various gullible and stupid European nobility.

00:10:21.449 --> 00:10:35.740
Thanks for watching.

